// pass_table.def — the ONE declarative table of every GPU command the sim
// records, and the thing Simulation::Encode* iterates to record it.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
// --------------------
// Phase 2b of docs/PLAN_vulkan_port.md. Dawn generates every hazard barrier in
// the tick automatically from bind-group usage. Vulkan does not: phase 3
// hand-writes ~60 read-after-write hops on one 512 MiB buffer, and a barrier
// that is too weak is not a crash — it is a race whose outcome depends on how a
// particular GPU schedules workgroups, i.e. a world hash that matches on the
// RTX 3060 Ti and diverges on the next vendor (CLAUDE.md rule 1).
//
// The design thesis (docs/vulkan_barrier_graph.md §1.1) is that barriers are
// GENERATED from this table and never hand-written at a call site. That is only
// worth anything if the table is FAITHFUL — if it says a pass reads `voxels`
// when it does not, phase 3 emits a barrier for a hazard that is not there
// (harmless); if it OMITS a read that happens, phase 3 emits nothing and the
// hazard is unsynchronized (a silent cross-vendor desync).
//
// Two mechanisms keep it faithful, and both depend on this being ONE list:
//
//   1. The recorder iterates it. Simulation::EncodeTick and friends do not
//      record commands directly any more — they walk the rows below. A row that
//      is wrong about its pipeline or its dispatch is wrong about what actually
//      runs, so the selftest catches it. The table cannot drift from the
//      recording because it IS the recording.
//   2. scripts/check_pass_table.py diffs each row's declared R/W sets against
//      the bindings its WGSL entry point actually touches, via a call-graph walk
//      ROOTED at that entry point (docs/vulkan_barrier_graph.md §6.1). It runs
//      from the PostToolUse hook on every .wgsl and on this file.
//
// Same play as src/sim/tuning_params.def: two lists that must agree are a
// silent bug, one list plus a generator is not. And the same standing
// obligation — a tick-path edit that changes a kernel's bindings updates this
// file in the same commit, or the checker fails (barrier_graph §8).
//
// HOW TO READ A ROW
// -----------------
//   PASS(name, passGroup, table, pipe, kind, dispatch..., groups, dynOff,
//        cond, repeat, uses)
//
//   name      stable identity.
//   passGroup which ComputePassEncoder the row is recorded into, and the label
//             that pass opens with (the PassTimer label under --measure).
//             Consecutive compute rows sharing a group string go into ONE pass,
//             exactly as the hand-written recorder did; nullptr on Fill/Copy
//             rows, which are encoder-level and end any open pass. The splits
//             are the thing phase 2b must NOT change, so they are stated rather
//             than inferred from adjacency.
//   table     which command buffer this row belongs to (PT_TICK, PT_WORLDGEN,
//             PT_GENLIST, PT_LOADRESET, PT_HASHONLY, PT_FARFILL). One table per
//             Encode* entry point, per barrier_graph §2.5's "a code path that
//             submits a command buffer gets a table".
//   pipe      PIPE_* selector into Simulation's pipeline members; PIPE_NONE for
//             Fill/Copy rows.
//   kind      K_COMPUTE (direct dispatch), K_INDIRECT (DispatchWorkgroupsIndirect),
//             K_FILL (ClearBuffer), K_COPY (CopyBufferToBuffer).
//   groups    GRP_SIM (simBG_ + dynamic passUBO offset), GRP_SLIM_PART
//             (simSlimBG_ + particleBG_), GRP_SLIM_FAR (simSlimBG_ + farBG_),
//             GRP_NONE for Fill/Copy.
//   cond      C_* predicate, evaluated on the CPU before recording. A row whose
//             condition is false is SKIPPED ENTIRELY — it records nothing and
//             (phase 3) touches no buffer's last-access state. barrier_graph
//             §3.9/§7.5: this is the only correct handling, and it is why
//             barriers must be computed at record time against live state and
//             never precomputed per adjacent table-index pair.
//   repeat    1 normally. 54 on the CA row — the 27 colors x 2 gravity
//             substeps, dispatched with a per-iteration dynamic passUBO offset.
//
// USES: R(b) read, W(b) written-not-read, RW(b) read+written, A(b) atomic RMW
// (READ|WRITE for barrier purposes — Vulkan has no atomic-specific access
// flag), U(b) uniform read, I(b) indirect-args read, TR(b) transfer read,
// TW(b) transfer write.
//
// `uses` is the union of the bindings the ENTRY POINT actually touches, NOT the
// bind-group layout. simBGL_ binds 17 buffers; sim_step.wgsl declares 8 and the
// table lists 8. Declaring the whole layout would insert barriers on occupancy,
// pick, hash and cellOps in the CA loop that are pure cost with no hazard
// behind them. The checker asserts layout ⊇ used, never layout == used.
//
// THE CA ROW IS NOT AN OPTIMIZATION TARGET. Its 54 iterations must never
// overlap, and that is a requirement of the 3x3x3 COLOR LATTICE, not of memory
// visibility (barrier_graph §3.6/§7.1). Within one dispatch every acting cell
// shares one colorPhase, so acting cells are >=3 apart on every axis while
// writes reach <=1 cell, and destination writes are provably disjoint. That
// disjointness holds within a color phase and nowhere else. Two iterations
// running concurrently are two DIFFERENT colors running concurrently, and cells
// of adjacent colors are adjacent by construction: their writes collide and the
// winner is whichever workgroup arrived first — which CLAUDE.md rule 1 bans by
// name. Batching iterations, merging "redundant" barriers between them, or
// splitting them across queues is the removal of rule 1, not a perf win.
//
// clang-format off

// ============================================================ PT_TICK ======
// Simulation::EncodeTick. Order below is the record order, verbatim.
// barrier_graph §2.4.

// ---- phase 0: pre-pass fills (simulation.cpp, top of EncodeTick) ----------
// DirtyOut is cleared every tick: it accumulates "written this tick" marks.
// argsStage is the compaction target; note the fill only zeroes the COUNT —
// sim_compact atomicStores args[1] and args[2] itself, so dispatchArgs.y/.z
// come from the SHADER. The fill->dispatch WAW is therefore load-bearing for
// dispatch VALIDITY, not merely count accuracy: lose it and the args read
// {count, 0, 0}, i.e. zero workgroups and a silently frozen world
// (barrier_graph §7.3).
PASS(fill_dirtyOut, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(DirtyOut)))
PASS(fill_argsStage, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(ArgsStage)))
PASS(fill_claim, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(TW(Claim)))
PASS(fill_expMask, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_EXP, 1,
     USES(TW(ExpMask)))
PASS(fill_hash, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_HASH, 1,
     USES(TW(Hash)))

// ---- phase 1: prep (one ComputePassEncoder today: mutate+explode+compact) --
// D_OPS/D_CELLS/D_EXP/D_SPAWN are dispatch-extent selectors resolved from the
// tick's counts at record time (see PassDispatchExtent in pass_table.cpp).
PASS(mutate, "prep(mutate+explode+compact)", PT_TICK, PIPE_MUTATE,      K_COMPUTE, D_OPS,4,4,   GRP_SIM,       DYN_ZERO, C_OPS, 1,
     USES(R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(OpsBuf) RW(Voxels) A(DirtyIn) A(DirtyOut)
          R(PageTable) A(PageFaults)))
// No R(Materials): `cells` writes the authored word straight into the grid and
// never consults the material table (unlike `main`, which does). The barrier
// doc's T11 row lists materials; the rooted walk says otherwise and the walk is
// right — check_pass_table.py caught this.
PASS(mutateCells, "prep(mutate+explode+compact)", PT_TICK, PIPE_MUTATE_CELLS,K_COMPUTE, D_CELLS,1,1, GRP_SIM,       DYN_ZERO, C_CELLS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(CellOps) RW(Voxels) A(DirtyIn) A(DirtyOut)
          R(PageTable) A(PageFaults)))
// mark reads the neighborhood, apply writes it. The two-phase split is what
// makes the explosion kernel race-free (CLAUDE.md build gotchas); it only works
// if the barrier between them exists, which under Dawn was free.
// read-only in practice: `voxels` is read_write for the module because `apply`
// writes it; `mark` is the pure-read half of that split and must stay so — a
// kernel that both reads and writes this neighborhood races itself.
PASS(explodeMark, "prep(mutate+explode+compact)", PT_TICK, PIPE_EXPLODE_MARK, K_COMPUTE, D_EXP,D_EXPWG,D_EXPWG,  GRP_SLIM_PART, DYN_NONE, C_EXP, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(ExpOps) W(ExpMask) A(DirtyIn) A(DirtyOut)
          R(PageTable)))
// read-only in practice: `expMask` is read_write for the module because `mark`
// writes it. `apply` consumes the mask `mark` produced and never amends it.
PASS(explodeApply, "prep(mutate+explode+compact)", PT_TICK, PIPE_EXPLODE_APPLY,K_COMPUTE, D_EXP,D_EXPWG,D_EXPWG,  GRP_SLIM_PART, DYN_NONE, C_EXP, 1,
     USES(R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(ExpOps) R(ExpMask) RW(Voxels) A(DirtyIn) A(DirtyOut)
          A(ParticleCounts) W(ParticlesRead) R(PageTable) A(PageFaults)))
// CPU particle spawns (debris shatter) append to the READ page here, before
// args1 sizes the integrate dispatch — same page explosion ejecta uses.
PASS(particleSpawn, "prep(mutate+explode+compact)", PT_TICK, PIPE_P_SPAWN,     K_COMPUTE, D_SPAWN,1,1,GRP_SLIM_PART, DYN_NONE, C_SPAWN, 1,
     USES(R(SpawnOps) U(TickUBO) A(ParticleCounts) W(ParticlesRead)))
// compact dirtyIn -> dense chunk list + indirect args, AFTER mutate/explode so
// freshly touched chunks simulate this tick.
PASS(compact, "prep(mutate+explode+compact)", PT_TICK, PIPE_COMPACT,      K_COMPUTE, D_CHUNKS64,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(R(DirtyIn) W(DirtyList) A(ArgsStage)))

// ---- phase 2: indirect staging -------------------------------------------
// The indirect-only args buffer is never bound in any bind group: Dawn forbids
// indirect + bound-writable usage of one buffer in the same pass, and
// sim_step.wgsl deliberately omits binding 13 to keep argsStage outside the CA
// pass's usage scope. v1 keeps the staging copy (barrier_graph §4.10).
PASS(copy_argsStage_dispatchArgs, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 0,0,12, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TR(ArgsStage) TW(DispatchArgs)))

// ---- phase 3: the CA loop -------------------------------------------------
// 27 colors x 2 gravity substeps, one workgroup per dirty chunk. A settled
// world dispatches zero workgroups here (DESIGN.md §11). DYN_CA feeds iteration
// k the passUBO slice at k * kPassStride — which is where colorPhase and
// substep come from, i.e. what makes each iteration a DIFFERENT color. See the
// lattice note in this file's header before touching repeat.
//
// dirtyList is declared read_write at binding 12 in sim_step.wgsl but the entry
// point only READS it (dirtyList[wg.x] at the top of main).
// read-only in practice: declared read_write only because WGSL cannot mix
// access modes for one binding across the module's entry points.
PASS(ca, "ca(54 color x substep)", PT_TICK, PIPE_STEP, K_INDIRECT, IND_DISPATCHARGS,0,0, GRP_SIM, DYN_CA, C_ALWAYS, 54,
     USES(R(Materials) U(TickUBO) U(PassUBO) R(Reactions) R(DirtyList) I(DispatchArgs)
          RW(Voxels) A(DirtyOut) A(Support) R(PageTable) A(PageFaults)))

// ---- phase 4: particles ---------------------------------------------------
// Runs after the CA so flights see settled ground. Grid writes land in
// dirtyOut, which the occupancy update below already covers.
PASS(particleArgs1, "particleArgs1", PT_TICK, PIPE_P_ARGS1, K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1, GRP_SLIM_PART, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(A(ParticleCounts) U(TickUBO) W(PArgsStage)))
PASS(copy_pArgs_pDispatch1, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 16,0,12, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(TR(PArgsStage) TW(PDispatchArgs)))
// No A(DirtyOut): `markDirtyNext` is the only writer of dirtyOut in this module
// and it is called only from `resolve` (sim_particle.wgsl:242, :265), never from
// `integrate`. The barrier doc's T42 row lists dirtyOut; the rooted walk says
// otherwise and the walk is right — check_pass_table.py caught this.
// read-only in practice: `voxels` is read_write for the module because
// `resolve` writes it when a particle rejoins the grid. `integrate` only tests
// cells for blockage along the flight path.
PASS(particleIntegrate, "particleIntegrate+args2", PT_TICK, PIPE_P_INTEGRATE, K_INDIRECT, IND_PDISPATCHARGS,0,0,
     GRP_SLIM_PART, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) RW(ParticlesRead) I(PDispatchArgs)
          W(ParticlesWrite) A(ParticleCounts) A(Claim) R(PageTable)))
PASS(particleArgs2, "particleIntegrate+args2", PT_TICK, PIPE_P_ARGS2, K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1, GRP_SLIM_PART, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(A(ParticleCounts) U(TickUBO) W(PArgsStage)))
// This second copy overwrites the args the integrate dispatch above FETCHED —
// a WAR against an in-flight indirect read, which is the case an implementer
// skips ("nothing reads it after the write"). barrier_graph §7.2.
PASS(copy_pArgs_pDispatch2, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 16,0,12, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(TR(PArgsStage) TW(PDispatchArgs)))
PASS(copy_pArgs_drawArgs, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 0,0,16, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(TR(PArgsStage) TW(DrawArgs)))
PASS(particleResolve, "particleResolve", PT_TICK, PIPE_P_RESOLVE, K_INDIRECT, IND_PDISPATCHARGS,0,0,
     GRP_SLIM_PART, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(R(Materials) U(TickUBO) A(Claim) A(ParticleCounts) I(PDispatchArgs)
          RW(Voxels) RW(ParticlesWrite) A(DirtyOut) R(PageTable) A(PageFaults)))

// ---- phase 5a: hash tick (whole-world scan; also refreshes occupancy) ------
PASS(occupancyFull, "occupancyFull+pick(hashTick)", PT_TICK, PIPE_OCCUPANCY, K_COMPUTE, D_CHUNKS,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_HASH, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) W(Occupancy) A(Hash) R(PageTable)))
PASS(pick_hash, "occupancyFull+pick(hashTick)", PT_TICK, PIPE_PICK,      K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1,       GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_HASH, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(RenderUBO) W(Pick) R(PageTable)))

// ---- phase 5b: dirty tick -------------------------------------------------
// Occupancy update over only the chunks written this tick: compact the dirtyOut
// flags (a superset of every voxel write) and dispatch indirect.
PASS(fill_argsStage2, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_DIRTYTICK, 1,
     USES(TW(ArgsStage)))
PASS(compactNext, "compactNext", PT_TICK, PIPE_COMPACT_NEXT, K_COMPUTE, D_CHUNKS64,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_DIRTYTICK, 1,
     USES(R(DirtyOut) W(DirtyList) A(ArgsStage)))
PASS(copy_argsStage_dispatchArgs2, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 0,0,12, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_DIRTYTICK, 1,
     USES(TR(ArgsStage) TW(DispatchArgs)))
// dirtyList declared read_write at binding 12 in sim_occupancy.wgsl, read only
// by mainDirty.
// read-only in practice: shared module-scope declaration with the compaction
// entry points, which do write it.
PASS(occupancyDirty, "occupancyDirty+pick", PT_TICK, PIPE_OCCUPANCY_DIRTY, K_INDIRECT, IND_DISPATCHARGS,0,0,
     GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_DIRTYTICK, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) R(DirtyList) I(DispatchArgs) W(Occupancy)
          R(PageTable)))
PASS(pick_dirty, "occupancyDirty+pick", PT_TICK, PIPE_PICK, K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_DIRTYTICK, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(RenderUBO) W(Pick) R(PageTable)))
// Far-field phase 2: downsample the same compacted dirtyOut list into the
// cascades, so edits stay visible after the player walks away (render-only
// derived data — DESIGN.md §9). Rides the SAME indirect args as the occupancy
// update, so a settled world dispatches nothing. Separate pass: it uses farPL_
// (slim group 0 + far group 1), not simPL_.
// NOTE: hash ticks skip this — they take the whole-world occupancy branch and
// never compact dirtyOut, so there is no work list. A chunk edited on a hash
// tick is still dirty the next tick and downsamples then.
// read-only in practice: `voxels` is read_write for the module because
// `genChunk` (main/list) writes it. `fardown` only samples the live grid to
// downsample into the cascades — far-field is derived, render-only data and
// never writes back (DESIGN.md §9).
PASS(farDown, "farDown", PT_TICK, PIPE_FAR_DOWN, K_INDIRECT, IND_DISPATCHARGS,0,0,
     GRP_SLIM_FAR, DYN_NONE, C_DIRTYTICK, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) U(FarUBO) R(DirtyList) I(DispatchArgs)
          A(FarVox) A(FarOcc) R(PageTable)))

// ---- phase 6: MLS-MPM fluid spawn (docs/PLAN_mpm_fluids.md prototype) ------
// CPU spawn-op stream -> particles, once per tick, BEFORE the substep table
// (PT_FLUID) that EncodeTick records kFluidSubsteps times after this table.
// No atomics: the append base (tickUBO.fluidBase) and count are CPU-owned, so
// slot assignment is a pure function of the op index.
PASS(fluidSpawn, "fluidSpawn", PT_TICK, PIPE_FLUID_SPAWN, K_COMPUTE, D_FLUIDSPAWN,1,1,
     GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_FLUIDSPAWN, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(FluidSpawnOps) W(FluidParticles)))

// ========================================================= PT_WORLDGEN =====
// Simulation::EncodeWorldgen. barrier_graph §2.5.1. The fills of dirty[0]/[1]
// DO hazard against the dispatch — genChunk atomicStores both — so "no hazards
// among them" applies only to the fills against each other.
PASS(wg_fill_dirty0, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Dirty0)))
PASS(wg_fill_dirty1, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Dirty1)))
PASS(wg_fill_hash, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Hash)))
PASS(wg_fill_support, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Support)))
PASS(wg_fill_pCounts, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(ParticleCounts)))
PASS(wg_fill_claim, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Claim)))
// no ghost particles: drawArgs retains the last active tick's instance count
PASS(wg_fill_drawArgs, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(DrawArgs)))
// one workgroup per slot chunk; occupancy + dirty flags computed in-kernel
// C_DENSEWG, not C_ALWAYS: under --residency paged this whole-world dispatch
// is replaced by batched worldgenList submits (PLAN_page_table.md §3.5c),
// because genChunk writes all 4,096 cells of every slot it touches and a
// kernel cannot allocate — all 32,768 at once would need a dense pool. The
// FILL rows above it still run in both modes: they clear the transient
// buffers (hash, support, particle counts, both dirty pages) that worldgen
// resets regardless of how the voxels get written.
PASS(worldgen, "worldgen", PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_WORLDGEN, K_COMPUTE, D_CHUNKS,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_DENSEWG, 1,
     USES(R(Materials) U(TickUBO) W(Voxels) W(Occupancy) A(DirtyIn) A(DirtyOut)
          R(PageTable) A(PageFaults)))

// ========================================================== PT_GENLIST =====
// Simulation::EncodeGenList — its own encoder, submitted MID-FRAME between
// ticks from Stream::FillSlots. Binds simBG_ (all 17 bindings) but
// worldgen:list/genChunk touch six of them; the `uses` set is the six.
// genChunk atomicStores both dirtyIn and dirtyOut while the concurrently
// in-flight tick's markDirty/markBoth atomically write the same two buffers —
// a cross-submit WAW against a shader atomic, covered by barrier_graph §3.4's
// head-of-command-buffer global barrier, not by anything in this row.
PASS(worldgenList, "worldgenList", PT_GENLIST, PIPE_WORLDGEN_LIST, K_COMPUTE, D_GENCOUNT,1,1,
     GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_GENCOUNT, 1,
     USES(R(GenList) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) W(Voxels) W(Occupancy) A(DirtyIn) A(DirtyOut)
          R(PageTable) A(PageFaults)))

// ======================================================== PT_LOADRESET =====
// Simulation::EncodeLoadReset. barrier_graph §2.5.3: clears transient state
// (hash/particles/claims) and rebuilds occupancy over freshly uploaded voxels.
// The caller has already written the voxel + dirty buffers (worldio.cpp) — one
// of the two sanctioned MutationQueue bypasses (CLAUDE.md rule 3).
PASS(lr_fill_hash, nullptr, PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Hash)))
PASS(lr_fill_support, nullptr, PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Support)))
PASS(lr_fill_pCounts, nullptr, PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(ParticleCounts)))
PASS(lr_fill_claim, nullptr, PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Claim)))
PASS(lr_fill_drawArgs, nullptr, PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(DrawArgs)))
PASS(lr_occupancyFull, "occupancyFull(loadReset)", PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_OCCUPANCY, K_COMPUTE, D_CHUNKS,1,1,
     GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) W(Occupancy) A(Hash) R(PageTable)))

// ========================================================= PT_HASHONLY =====
// Simulation::EncodeHashOnly — standalone whole-world hash pass (save/load
// verification). barrier_graph §2.5.4.
PASS(ho_fill_hash, nullptr, PT_HASHONLY, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Hash)))
PASS(ho_occupancyFull, "occupancyFull(hashOnly)", PT_HASHONLY, PIPE_OCCUPANCY, K_COMPUTE, D_CHUNKS,1,1,
     GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) W(Occupancy) A(Hash) R(PageTable)))

// ========================================================== PT_FARFILL =====
// Simulation::EncodeFarFill. Encoded into the tick's command buffer AFTER
// EncodeTick today, and also driven standalone in a loop by --shot's far-field
// drain (barrier_graph §2.5.6). farCount travels INSIDE tickUBO, not as a
// dispatch dimension, so in the standalone loop the upload->dispatch RAW on
// tickUBO is load-bearing PER ITERATION.
//
// farVox/farOcc carry a WAW against farDown, which wrote them earlier in the
// same command buffer with a DIFFERENT atomic idiom (atomicAnd/atomicOr vs this
// row's full-word atomicStore). "They're both atomic" is not a reason to relax
// the barrier: atomics guarantee per-operation atomicity, not visibility of one
// dispatch's results to the next. barrier_graph §3.8/§7.8.
PASS(farFill, "farFill", PT_FARFILL, PIPE_FAR_FILL, K_COMPUTE, D_FARCOUNT,1,1,
     GRP_SLIM_FAR, DYN_NONE, C_FARCOUNT, 1,
     USES(R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(FarList) U(FarUBO) A(FarVox) A(FarOcc)))

// =========================================================== PT_FLUID ======
// ONE MLS-MPM SUBSTEP (docs/PLAN_mpm_fluids.md; sim_fluid.wgsl). Recorded
// kFluidSubsteps times per tick from EncodeTick, into the TICK's command
// buffer, only when fluid particles exist — a world that never placed the
// prototype fluid records none of this (rule 2). The spawn row is NOT here:
// it runs once per tick and lives at the end of PT_TICK below.
//
// The recorder's last-access tracker persists across RecordTable calls within
// one command buffer, so repeating this table generates the inter-substep
// barriers (grid WAW into the next clear, particle RAW into the next mark)
// exactly like intra-table ones. Determinism does not ride on any of these
// conditions: every row is C_ALWAYS within the table, and whether the table
// is recorded at all is a pure function of the CPU-owned particle count.
//
// The scratch grid is rebuilt from scratch every substep: fill zeroes the
// chunk->block map, mark flags chunks under particle support (atomicOr — the
// final map state is order-independent), alloc assigns block indices by a
// single-workgroup scan in slot order (deterministic) and emits the node-pass
// dispatch args, clear zeroes exactly the active blocks' accumulators.
PASS(fluid_fill_blockMap, nullptr, PT_FLUID, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(FluidBlockMap)))
// read-only in practice: `fluidParticles` is read_write for the module because
// spawn and g2p write it. mark only reads positions to flag chunks.
PASS(fluidMark, "fluid(substep)", PT_FLUID, PIPE_FLUID_MARK, K_COMPUTE, D_FLUIDP,1,1, GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(FluidParticles) A(FluidBlockMap)))
PASS(fluidAlloc, "fluid(substep)", PT_FLUID, PIPE_FLUID_ALLOC, K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1, GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(RW(FluidBlockMap) W(FluidBlockList) W(FluidArgsStage)))
// Same staging-copy shape as argsStage -> dispatchArgs: the indirect-only
// buffer is never bound in any bind group.
PASS(copy_fluidArgs, nullptr, PT_FLUID, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 0,0,12, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TR(FluidArgsStage) TW(FluidDispatchArgs)))
PASS(fluidClear, "fluid(substep)", PT_FLUID, PIPE_FLUID_CLEAR, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDARGS,0,0,
     GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(W(FluidGrid) I(FluidDispatchArgs)))
// P2G's scatter is the plan's §5.1 bet made real: i32 atomicAdd accumulation,
// associative, so the sum cannot depend on scheduling. The fill->mark->alloc
// chain above already ordered the map; the barrier p2g1 needs is the
// clear->scatter WAW on fluidGrid, generated from the two rows' uses.
// read-only in practice: `fluidParticles` is read_write for the module (spawn
// and g2p write it); p2g1 only reads particle state to scatter it. Likewise
// `fluidBlockMap` is written by mark/alloc; p2g only atomicLoads the mapping.
PASS(fluidP2g1, "fluid(substep)", PT_FLUID, PIPE_FLUID_P2G, K_COMPUTE, D_FLUIDP,1,1, GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(FluidParticles) R(FluidBlockMap) A(FluidGrid)))
// p2g2 is the density-EOS half of the grantkot two-pass P2G: it needs p2g1's
// COMPLETE mass field (the A->A WAW barrier between these rows orders it),
// gathers density per particle, and scatters stress as momentum. Within the
// pass it reads only mass words and adds only to momentum words — disjoint,
// and both order-independent. RW on fluidParticles is real: p2g2 writes
// p.density (render shading reads it).
// read-only in practice: `fluidBlockMap` is written by mark/alloc; p2g2 only
// atomicLoads the mapping, exactly like p2g1 above.
PASS(fluidP2g2, "fluid(substep)", PT_FLUID, PIPE_FLUID_P2G2, K_COMPUTE, D_FLUIDP,1,1, GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) RW(FluidParticles) R(FluidBlockMap) A(FluidGrid)))
// Terrain boundary conditions read the LIVE voxel buffer through voxWordAt —
// carve under a pool and the wall simply stops being there next substep
// (plan §6.1). Reads only; the fluid never writes a voxel, which is what keeps
// the world hash out of this system's blast radius.
// read-only in practice: `fluidBlockList` is read_write for the module because
// alloc writes it; the grid update only reads its block's chunk slot.
PASS(fluidGridUp, "fluid(substep)", PT_FLUID, PIPE_FLUID_GRIDUP, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDARGS,0,0,
     GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(PageTable) R(FluidBlockList)
          RW(FluidGrid) I(FluidDispatchArgs)))
// Pure gather for the fluid itself: each thread writes only its own particle,
// so g2p cannot race itself (the mark/apply split rule does not bite here).
// The splash coupling adds two writes: fast free-surface particles append
// PFLAG_MICRO droplets to the ballistic particle system's WRITE page (counts
// atomicAdd + slot store — the same order-independent append integrate uses;
// nothing keys on the slot). This runs AFTER particleResolve in the tick, so
// the appends land beyond the resolved count and fly next tick.
// read-only in practice: `fluidBlockMap` is written by mark/alloc and
// `fluidGrid` by clear/p2g/gridUpdate; g2p only atomicLoads both.
PASS(fluidG2p, "fluid(substep)", PT_FLUID, PIPE_FLUID_G2P, K_COMPUTE, D_FLUIDP,1,1, GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) RW(FluidParticles) R(FluidBlockMap) R(FluidGrid)
          W(ParticlesWrite) A(ParticleCounts)))

// clang-format on
